<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Pay rise claim</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>PAYE tax bands for resident individuals, from 1 July 2023 (Kenya Revenue Authority; Finance Act 2023):</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Monthly taxable pay (Ksh)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Rate</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">First \(24\,000\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(10\%\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Next \(8333\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(25\%\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Next \(467\,667\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(30\%\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Next \(300\,000\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(32.5\%\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Above \(800\,000\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(35\%\)</td></tr></table><p>Personal relief: Ksh \(2400\) a month, taken off the tax. PAYE is never negative.</p><p>{@nm@}'s taxable pay rises from Ksh \({@ksh(X1)@}\) to Ksh \({@ksh(X2)@}\) a month. The other deductions on the payslip do not change. {@nm@} says: <q>Now I am in the \(30\%\) band, so all my pay is taxed at \(30\%\) and I will take home less than before.</q></p><p>(a) Find the PAYE before the rise.</p><p>Ksh [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p><p>(b) Find the PAYE after the rise.</p><p>Ksh [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p><p>(c) Which statement is right?</p><p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p><p>(d) By how much does take-home pay change? Type a negative number for a fall.</p><p>Ksh [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Before: tax \(= 2400 + 25\% \times ({@ksh(X1)@} - 24\,000) = {@ksh(t1)@}\); PAYE \(= {@ksh(t1)@} - 2400 = {@ksh(p1)@}\).</p><p>(b) After: tax \(= 2400 + 2083.25 + 30\% \times ({@ksh(X2)@} - 32\,333) = {@ksh(t2)@}\); PAYE \(= {@ksh(p2)@}\).</p><p>(c) Each slice of pay is taxed at its own band's rate, so a rise never lowers take-home pay. Only the part above \(32\,333\) pays \(30\%\); personal relief stays Ksh \(2400\).</p><p>(d) The pay rises by \({@ksh(rs)@}\) and PAYE by \({@ksh(p2)@} - {@ksh(p1)@} = {@ksh(pc)@}\), so take-home pay rises by \({@ksh(rs)@} - {@ksh(pc)@} = {@ksh(inc)@}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
